-- title:
--   Off-diagonal vanishing in the linear part of an inertial automorphism
-- statement:
--   Arithmetic data. A prime $q$, a natural number $M'$ with $M' \neq 0$ and $q \nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell$ with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. A field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of level $\{q\ell\}$, an element $\zeta \in L$ that is a primitive $q$-th root of unity, an element $\xi \in L$ that is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity, and the relation $\zeta = \xi^{\ell}$.
--
--   Level and function field. A subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times$ subject to `hH₁`: $H_1$ is the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22), the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$ (the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$), with the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/\ell)^\times$. An intermediate field $K$ of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ subject to `hK`: $K$ is [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), that is, the $L$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ generated by the coefficientwise image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion function field of $X_{H_1}$ of level $q^2M'$ over $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   Base ring and $j$. A discrete valuation ring $A$ that is a domain, with an algebra structure over which $L$ is its fraction field, such that $q \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ (`hAq`) and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A \to L$ (`hζA`); an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $A \to L \to K$; an element $j \in K$, nonzero, whose Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion $\mathsf{q}^{-1}\cdot j_{\mathrm{num}}$ of the modular invariant; and a uniformiser $\varpi$ of $A$, i.e. $\mathfrak{m}_A = (\varpi)$.
--
--   Geometric point. The scheme [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) is the pushout of the two affine charts $\operatorname{Spec}$ of `chartAlgFin A K j` (the elements of $K$ integral over $A[j]$) and $\operatorname{Spec}$ of `chartAlgInf A K j` (the elements integral over $A[j^{-1}]$) along the middle chart. A point $z$ of this scheme is given, together with the element $\varpi_z$ of the stalk at $z$ obtained (`hϖz`) as the germ at $z$ of the image of $\varpi$ under the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and the hypothesis `hz` that $\varpi_z$ lies in the maximal ideal of that stalk. A point $y$ of $\operatorname{Spec}$ `chartAlgFin A K j` is given with $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}(y) = z$. Supersingularity at $y$ (`hss`): for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from `chartAlgFin A K j` to $\Omega$ with kernel $y.\mathrm{asIdeal}$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero point killed by $q$.
--
--   Drinfeld chart witness. A complete discrete valuation ring $W$ (a domain, adically complete for its maximal ideal) and a ring homomorphism $\sigma : A \to W$ with $\mathfrak{m}_W = (\sigma\varpi)$; power series $f, u, v \in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u$ and $v$ units and $f - (X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1) \in (X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf`, with [`DrinfeldCurve.LocalChart.drinfeldForm q W`](def/DrinfeldCurve_LocalChart.html#L18) the displayed Drinfeld form); and a ring isomorphism
--   $$e : \widehat{\mathcal{O}}_z \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; S := W[[X_0,X_1]]/\bigl(C(\sigma\varpi)\,v - f\,u\bigr),$$
--   where $\widehat{\mathcal{O}}_z$ is the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ for its maximal ideal. Throughout, `toC` denotes the map from the stalk to its completion, `mkS` the quotient map onto $S$, and `germY` the canonical ring homomorphism from `chartAlgFin A K j` to the stalk at $z$ (the germ at $z$ over the open image of $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$, composed with the identifications of the chart with the spectrum of its coordinate ring).
--
--   Normalisation of constants (`hconst`): for every $a \in A$, the isomorphism $e$ sends the image in $\widehat{\mathcal{O}}_z$ of the germ at $z$ of $a$ (via the structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$) to the class of the constant series $C(\sigma a)$.
--
--   Level equivariance (`hlin`). For every $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M')$ and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L q ζ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29) — that is: for every weight $k$, every pair of modular forms $f_0, g_0$ for $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ of weight $k$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f, p_g$ and $p_g$ nonzero in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$, every $x \in K$ whose Laurent expansion is the coefficientwise image of $p_f/p_g$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ with $\iota(\zeta) = e^{2\pi i/q}$, one has $\mathrm{coeffMap}\,\iota(\tau x)$ times the $q$-expansion of $g_0 \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$ equal to the $q$-expansion of $f_0 \mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\gamma^{-1}$, where $\mathrm{conjElemN}\,q\,\delta$ is the matrix $\begin{pmatrix}\delta_{00} & \delta_{01}/q\\ q\,\delta_{10} & \delta_{11}\end{pmatrix}$ — and for every proof that $\tau$ maps `chartAlgFin A K j` into itself, if $\tau(a) - a \in y.\mathrm{asIdeal}$ for all $a$ in that chart algebra, then there exist a ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$, an element $c \in W$ and a matrix $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ such that: $\theta$ is induced by $\tau$, in the sense that $\theta(e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(a)))) = e(\mathrm{toC}(\mathrm{germY}(\tau a)))$ for all $a$ in the chart algebra; $\theta$ fixes the classes of all constants $C(w)$, $w \in W$; for each index $jj$, $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}(X_{jj})) - \mathrm{mkS}\bigl(\sum_{ii} C(M_{ii\,jj})X_{ii}\bigr)$ lies in the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mkS}(X_0), \mathrm{mkS}(X_1)$; $c^{q+1} - 1 \in \mathfrak{m}_W$; $M_{ii\,jj} - c\,\gamma_{ii\,jj} \in \mathfrak{m}_W$ for all $ii, jj$; if $\gamma_{11} \equiv 1 \pmod{\ell}$ then $c - 1 \in \mathfrak{m}_W$; and if $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau$ is not the identity on $K$ then $c - 1 \notin \mathfrak{m}_W$.
--
--   Conclusion. Under these hypotheses, with $S$, $\mathrm{mkS}$, $\mathrm{toC}$ and $\mathrm{germY}$ as above: for all ring automorphisms $\sigma_L$ of $L$ and $\sigma_A$ of $A$ such that $\sigma_A$ is compatible with $\sigma_L$ along $A \to L$ and $\sigma_A(a) - a \in \mathfrak{m}_A$ for every $a \in A$; for every ring automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ acting coefficientwise through $\sigma_L$, i.e. the Laurent expansion of $\tau x$ equals [`ModularCurve.coeffMap`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) $\sigma_L$ applied to that of $x$ for all $x \in K$; given that $\tau$ maps `chartAlgFin A K j` into itself, that $\tau^{-1}$ does likewise, and that $\tau(a) - a \in y.\mathrm{asIdeal}$ for every $a$ in the chart algebra; for every ring automorphism $\theta$ of $S$ and every ring automorphism $\sigma_W$ of $W$ such that $\theta$ is induced by $\tau$ through $e \circ \mathrm{toC} \circ \mathrm{germY}$, such that $\sigma_W \circ \sigma = \sigma \circ \sigma_A$, such that $\sigma_W(w) - w \in \mathfrak{m}_W$ for all $w \in W$, and such that $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}(C(w))) = \mathrm{mkS}(C(\sigma_W w))$ for all $w \in W$; and for every matrix $M \in \mathrm{Mat}_2(W)$ which is a linear part of $\theta$, in the sense that for each index $jj$ the difference $\theta(\mathrm{mkS}(X_{jj})) - \mathrm{mkS}\bigl(\sum_{ii} C(M_{ii\,jj})X_{ii}\bigr)$ lies in the square of the ideal generated by $\mathrm{mkS}(X_0)$ and $\mathrm{mkS}(X_1)$ — one has
--   $$M_{0\,1} \in \mathfrak{m}_W.$$
--
--   At a supersingular point of the integral model of $X_{H_1}$ of level $q^2M'$ (with the auxiliary $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-type condition at the guard prime $\ell \equiv 11 \bmod 12$), the completed local ring is presented as a Drinfeld chart $W[[X_0,X_1]]/(C(\sigma\varpi)v - fu)$ with $f$ congruent to $X_0X_1^q - X_0^qX_1$ modulo degree $q+2$; the two coordinates single out the two branches through the point. The statement says that an automorphism coming from an inertial element of the Galois group of the base, acting coefficientwise on $q$-expansions and semilinearly over $\sigma_W \equiv \mathrm{id}$, has upper-triangular-free linear part in the sense that its $(0,1)$ entry is not a unit, so that the branch structure is preserved; it is used in the analysis of the inertia action on the Drinfeld chart that follows it.
